WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.533
This week

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:01.801
was undoubtedly

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.303
one of the worst weeks

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.105
of Donald Trump's political career.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.574
And that comes after two very,

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.776
very bad weeks for the former president

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.978
In just the last 26 days

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.213
since he launched his bid

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:14.948
to return to the White House,

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:16.716
a special counsel was appointed.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.418
He had dinner with antisemites.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.253
Kanye West and Nick Fuentes,

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.421
Multiple associates

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.523
were ordered to testify about him.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.691
He recorded a video

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.959
for the families of jailed

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.861
January six rioters.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.162
He called for the termination

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:30.630
of the constitu ation.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.665
His company was convicted of tax fraud

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:33.500
and other

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.734
hand-picked candidate

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:37.404
in Georgia suffered a stinging defeat.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.472
And he hosted a prominent Q

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:40.740
and on backer.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.842
And more classified documents

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.944
were found in a storage unit.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.679
And that is just a partial list,

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.781
all of which has left

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.682
even his allies questioning

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.051
whether the former president

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.486
is finally becoming a liability.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.021
Now we can roll.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.323
The Star Wars credits for

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:59.459
about ten more minutes on that one.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.895
But the question is,

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.630
is there any reason to think

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.765
that this is not going to get worse

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.434
for the Republican Party

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.836
dealing with the shenanigans

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:10.837
from former president?

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.138
I mean, I think the bigger question is,

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.139
is there any reason to think

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:13.907
that the Republican Party

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:15.542
is actually going to break from him

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.509
to some degree?

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.044
And the reason I say that

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:18.778
is because

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.112
look, there's

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:19.813
no question

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.281
Republicans that we've all been speaking

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:22.348
to over the course of the last

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:22.782
three weeks

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.417
have been in a different place

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.585
than they probably had been

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.853
in the six years prior.

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.121
And yet over the course of six years,

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:28.721
I feel like we've had

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:30.356
50 of these moments, really like

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.558
this is going to be the time

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.293
where the grip finally loosens

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:34.294
and everybody starts

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:35.762
to move away from him.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.863
And so it's one of those

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.465
like I'll believe it when I see it,

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:40.300
because background quotes from lawmakers

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:40.934
are not going to be

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.168
the thing that triggers it.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:42.735
The reality,

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.737
though, is the biggest moment

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.306
here is the midterm elections,

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.041
because when Donald Trump loses

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.275
his power, it's when Republicans

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:49.609
no longer

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:51.144
think they can ride him to wins,

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.378
or most importantly,

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:53.379
when Republican voters

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:54.647
and the constituents that are calling

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.316
their offices are no longer saying

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.016
step by Trump

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.952
or we will primary you and you will lose,

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.153
this is the key. Yes.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.588
Well, that's that's everything.

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:02.722
All of Trump's power

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:03.923
emanates from the voters.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.859
And so I've done a bunch of focus groups

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.361
with Trump voters since the midterms.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:09.129
And here's the thing.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.131
They are not ready to break with Trump,

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.532
but they are ready to shop.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:12.899
They're ready

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.334
to look at some alternatives.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.502
And so, you know,

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:16.302
we asked people

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.470
hey, were you enthusiastic

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.605
about Donald Trump's announcement?

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:19.506
And they were like,

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.940
yeah, it's a

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:21.908
yeah, I'll vote for him

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:22.709
if he's the nominee.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.711
But they were much more interested

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.112
in seeing people like DeSantis

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:26.980
get in the race.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.448
They talk about Kristi Noem.

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:29.816
There's a real sense of

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.217
I think it's time to turn the page.

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.752
I think it's time to move on.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.053
And it's not it's not

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.088
you know, they're not pundits.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.422
They're not like, ooh, he's

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.690
he's costing us elections.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:38.358
It's that they think

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.692
he has too much baggage.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.227
They don't want to go through another

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.729
four years of kind of the orange

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:43.329
brand bad.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.031
Everybody's, you know, angry

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:45.598
all the time.

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:46.199
The polarization,

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:47.567
they think that they have a

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.168
lot of it, actually.

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:48.735
For them,

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.236
it it does come down to electability.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.271
They'd like to see somebody

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:52.138
that they think

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.006
can win

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.040
and can beat Joe Biden

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:54.607
and they're not sure

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:55.675
that's Trump anymore.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:56.643
I think people are also

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.378
just really tired.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:02:58.811
Yeah,

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.847
they're tired of the back and forth,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.649
the fights over Thanksgiving dinner,

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:03.616
all of it.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.552
But we did kind of this week

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:06.586
see a little bit

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.054
more of an inching toward

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.090
saying the T-word among some Republicans

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.158
and actually

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:12.992
being clearer

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.194
about Trump's role in all of this.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:14.794
Take a listen.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:17.830
The Republican

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:18.965
Party has to

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:21.301
really look deep into our soul.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.404
We have to, first of all, understand

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.039
that there's a price to be paid

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.774
whenever you have a Trump endorsement.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.542
The Democrats in many cases were able

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:30.376
to kind of

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.644
turn it into a choice election

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.980
because of of Trump's presence out there.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.249
So you know, was he a factor?

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:37.116
I don't think

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.419
there's any question about that.

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:41.721
Are you seeing a little bit

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:42.455
more of that on the Hill?

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:43.456
I mean, I think Asa

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.691
Hutchinson, not a huge surprise,

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.926
but John Thune being really clear to me

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.162
that stood out. Yeah.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.797
So Senator Thune has been some

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.632
he's the number two senator

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:54.601
in the Republican Party

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.102
he's been critical of Trump

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:57.170
for a couple of years,

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.339
but never explicitly saying

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:01.407
that I'm not going to support him.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:02.475
I sat down with him

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:03.009
and talked to him

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.410
for a 30 minute interview,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.512
and he still wouldn't say

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:07.947
that he would not support him

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:09.082
if he was the nominee.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.417
Even though he does not think Trump

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:11.951
should run again,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:12.952
he does not think he should be

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.520
the standard bearer of the party.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.422
And I flat out asked him,

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:19.892
why can't Republicans break from Trump?

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.028
Because we know it's the voters,

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.329
but it's one thing to hear

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.331
a Republican say it. And he said that.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:27.400
He said we have to be very careful

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.303
because Trump still has a lot of support

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.638
among the Republican base,

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:33.840
and that means that you can't win

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.042
a primary election without Trump,

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.477
but you also can't win

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:39.545
a general election with Trump.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:41.281
So that's the part

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:42.181
that's the challenge

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.516
that Republicans are in right now.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:44.183
And they're going to have

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:44.984
to make a decision

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:45.885
on how they handle this.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.487
It's hard, though, to

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:49.222
to to really believe that this

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:49.822
that Trump

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.557
may still not have a grip on the party.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.960
I go back to the weeks after January six

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:54.894
when you

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.929
when you saw so many Republican leaders

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.665
condemn him, say his name,

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.533
and then just a couple of weeks later,

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.467
you saw Kevin McCarthy

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.670
go to Mar a Lago as well.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.204
The political factor here

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:05.772
and just

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:07.573
the impact that he had on

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.409
the midterms is probably going to be

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:09.742
the thing

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:10.343
that continues

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.845
to really be a factor moving forward.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.380
Although, you know,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.481
his hand-picked candidate

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.382
in Georgia losing

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:17.884
also adds to that as well.

00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:19.452
And you hear from Mitt Romney

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.454
the other side of

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:22.088
this, which is that

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:24.223
it should not just be that Trump loses.

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:25.325
But that's the reason

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:25.992
that they step away.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.761
It should be other things. Take a listen.

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:29.896
It's going to be hard to

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.331
to knock him off as our nominee

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:32.432
if he became a nominee.

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:33.933
I think he loses again.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.401
And would you support him

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.936
if he's the nominee? Absolutely not.

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:39.138
And it's not just because he loses.

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:39.772
I mean, that's

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.808
that's my reason that I offer

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.043
to other people who are big fans of his.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:46.746
But but it's also he's

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:49.082
simply not a person who ought to have

00:05:49.082 --> 00:05:49.515
the reins

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.151
of the government of the United States.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:52.652
Really.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:53.553
And a great interview

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:54.320
that you did with him.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.389
But very few Republicans

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.124
are willing to say that he's an outlier.

00:05:58.124 --> 00:05:58.658
Yeah.

00:05:58.925 --> 00:05:59.659
Here's the thing, though.

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:01.527
Trump is at his weakest moment right

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:03.663
now, and he was weak after January 6th.

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:04.731
But the problem is, is

00:06:04.731 --> 00:06:06.032
what Republicans did in that moment

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.500
is Kevin McCarthy went down to Mar a Lago

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.601
and kind of resurrected him.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.103
Republicans have got to do

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:11.170
something about Trump.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.505
They have to say his name.

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:13.506
They have to go on offense.

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:14.240
If they don't want him

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.775
dragging down the party,

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:17.110
they have got to actually do

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:17.810
something about it.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.145
If they just sit there in silence

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:20.913
and hope that he sort of crumbles

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.015
under the weight of his own scandal.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:24.617
That's the movie we've all seen before.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:25.918
And that's the thing we know

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:26.853
doesn't just happen.

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.187
They're going to have to do something

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.555
that's it's the biggest thing.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.256
And it was

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:31.257
it was the thing after,

00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:31.457
you know,

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:32.492
calling for

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:34.093
dissolution of the Constitution,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:35.094
which is the entire

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.529
kind of animating factor

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:38.865
of the Republican Party, allegedly.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:39.932
And that wasn't enough

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.334
to get everybody to come out.

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:42.101
And you just felt like

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:43.069
we were doing a rerun.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:44.737
Serious point that was so critical here.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.205
There are a dozen Republicans

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:46.973
who are either weighing

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:47.740
presidential bids

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.041
or thinking about presidential bids,

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.710
and a great number of them

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.512
are the kind of the epitome of the well,

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:53.413
they have the policy

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:55.081
that Trump had that we liked,

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.449
and they have kind of the

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:58.584
aggressive responses that Trump had.

00:06:58.584 --> 00:06:59.886
But they don't have all the baggage.

00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:01.320
They don't have the anti-Semites

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.221
that they're hanging out with.

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:03.689
They're not calling for the dissolution

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:04.357
of the Constitution.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:05.625
There are real alternatives.

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:06.659
I think, for the first time

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:07.994
on the national stage.

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:09.095
Is that what causes

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.062
the turn

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:10.730
in the voters

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:11.731
that leads to the turn

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:13.766
and the lawmakers and policymakers?

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:14.867
I don't know.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:15.902
But that's the

00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:17.136
that's the wild card, I think.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:18.571
I think one of the biggest problems

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:19.305
for Republicans

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:20.072
is that a lot of them

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:20.840
are waiting for this

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.675
to just sort itself out.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.177
And I think your point

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:25.311
to your point, Sarah,

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:26.345
that's a great recipe

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.347
for Donald Trump getting 30% of the vote

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.082
and winning the Republican nomination.

